WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:04.690
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:09.590
Approximately two years ago, we covered a
work where a learning-based algorithm was

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:16.010
able to read the wifi signals in a room to
not only locate a person in a building, but

00:00:16.010 --> 00:00:19.170
even estimate their pose.

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:23.730
An additional property of this method was
that, as you see here, it does not look at

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:30.070
images, but radio signals, which also traverse
in the dark, and therefore, this pose estimation

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:33.230
also works well in poor lighting conditions.

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:38.890
Today’s paper offers a learning-based method
for a different, more practical data completion

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:42.510
problem, and it mainly works on image sequences.

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:47.600
You see, we can give this one a short image
sequence with obstructions, for instance,

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:48.960
the fence here.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:54.399
And it is able to find and remove this obstruction,
and not only that, but it can also show us

00:00:54.399 --> 00:00:57.260
what is exactly behind the fence!

00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:59.140
How is that even possible?

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:04.571
Well, note that we mentioned that the input
is not an image, but an image sequence, a

00:01:04.571 --> 00:01:06.590
short video if you will.

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:11.380
This contains the scene from different viewpoints,
and is one of the typical cases where if we

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:17.270
would give all this data to a human, this
human would take a long-long time, but would

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:22.869
be able to reconstruct what is exactly behind
the fence, because this data was visible from

00:01:22.869 --> 00:01:23.880
other viewpoints.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:29.630
But of course, clearly, this approach would
be prohibitively slow and expensive.

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:36.049
The cool thing here is that this learning-based
method is capable of doing this, automatically!

00:01:36.049 --> 00:01:38.609
But it does not stop there.

00:01:38.609 --> 00:01:44.079
I was really surprised to find out that it
even works for video outputs as well, so if

00:01:44.079 --> 00:01:48.469
you did not have a clear sight of that tiger
in the zoo, do not despair!

00:01:48.469 --> 00:01:50.959
Just use this method, and there you go.

00:01:50.959 --> 00:01:55.130
When looking at the results of techniques
like this, I always try to only look at the

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:59.409
output, and try to guess where the fence was
obstructing it.

00:01:59.409 --> 00:02:04.829
With many simpler, image inpainting techniques,
this is easy to tell if you look for it, but

00:02:04.829 --> 00:02:07.249
here, I can’t see a trace.

00:02:07.249 --> 00:02:08.249
Can you?

00:02:08.249 --> 00:02:09.720
Let me know in the comments.

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:15.730
Admittedly, the resolution of this video is
not very high, but the results look very reassuring.

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:21.569
It can also perform reflection removal, and
some of the input images are highly contaminated

00:02:21.569 --> 00:02:23.180
by these reflected objects.

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:25.450
Let’s have a look at some results!

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:31.620
You can see here how the technique decomposes
the input into two images, one with the reflection,

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:32.980
and one without.

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:38.409
The results are clearly not perfect, but they
are easily good enough to make my brain focus

00:02:38.409 --> 00:02:42.939
on the real background without being distracted
by the reflections.

00:02:42.939 --> 00:02:45.659
This was not the case with the input at all.

00:02:45.659 --> 00:02:46.670
Bravo!

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:52.040
This use case can also be extended for videos,
and I wonder how much temporal coherence I

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:54.099
can expect in the output.

00:02:54.099 --> 00:02:59.209
In other words, if the technique solves the
adjacent frames too differently, flickering

00:02:59.209 --> 00:03:04.510
is introduced to the video, and this effect
is the bane of many techniques that are otherwise

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:06.650
really good on still images.

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:07.690
Let’s have a look!

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:13.810
There is a tiny bit of flickering, but the
results are surprisingly consistent.

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:20.299
It also does quite well when compared to previous
methods, especially when we are able to provide

00:03:20.299 --> 00:03:23.360
multiple images as an input.

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:28.080
Now note that it says “ours, without online
optimization”.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:29.650
What could that mean?

00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:35.079
This online optimization step is a computationally
expensive way to further improve separation

00:03:35.079 --> 00:03:40.780
in the outputs, and with that, the authors
propose a quicker and a slower version of

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:41.780
the technique.

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:47.689
The one without the on-line optimization step
runs in just a few seconds, and if we add

00:03:47.689 --> 00:03:51.180
this step, we will have to wait approximately
15 minutes.

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:56.659
I had to read the table several times, because
researchers typically bring the best version

00:03:56.659 --> 00:04:01.219
of their technique to the comparisons, and
it is not the case here.

00:04:01.219 --> 00:04:04.420
Even the quicker version smokes the competition!

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:05.770
Loving it.

00:04:05.770 --> 00:04:10.120
Note if you have a look at the paper in the
video description, there are, of course, more

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:13.249
detailed comparisons against other methods
as well.

00:04:13.249 --> 00:04:18.569
If these AR glasses that we hear so much about
come to fruition in the next few years, having

00:04:18.569 --> 00:04:25.250
an algorithm for real-time glare, reflection
and obstruction removal would be beyond amazing.

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:27.411
We truly live in a science fiction world.

00:04:27.411 --> 00:05:04.430
What a time

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:15.970
to be alive!

00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:23.219
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

